
{"id":"51890051-6a09-4144-a271-1e395c499378","object":"chat.completion","created":1785015766,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task DATA-HDB-02. The problem describes a condensed tree stability selection algorithm. We need to process the input case payload, which contains min_cluster_size, clusters (list of objects with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), parent mapping, and other fields. The output must be a JSON object with clusters array and selected_cluster_ids array, matching the schema.\n\nWe need to:\n1. Validate required fields? The input schema includes min_cluster_size, clusters, parent, _case_nonce. We need to use these.\n2. Drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size. Those clusters are considered noise? But note: the parent mapping may refer to clusters that are dropped. We need to handle that: when dropping a cluster, its points? Actually, the algorithm: \"Condensed-tree construction\" – we need to build a condensed tree where clusters below min_cluster_size are considered as \"noise\" and their points are assigned to the nearest surviving ancestor? The problem statement: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" Also: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" This suggests that points from dropped clusters are reassigned? But the input provides per-cluster point_exit_lambdas and birth_lambda. A cluster's points have exit_lambdas relative to that cluster. If a cluster is dropped, those points should be assigned to the parent cluster (or maybe to the next surviving ancestor). The parent mapping indicates the hierarchical relationship. Typically in HDBSCAN condensed tree, clusters are nodes with size, birth lambda, and a list of exit lambdas for each point. When a cluster is below min_cluster_size, it is considered as part of its parent cluster (i.e., its points are merged into the parent). The stability of a cluster is sum over its own points (that have not been reassigned) of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Points that are reassigned to a surviving ancestor also contribute to that ancestor's stability? Actually, they are not separate; their exit_lambda relative to the ancestor is different? In standard HDBSCAN condensed tree, points have birth and death lambdas relative to the cluster hierarchy. When a child cluster is removed (e.g., because it's too small), the points in that child are considered as having died at the child's birth lambda? Or they are assigned to the parent? The problem statement: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" Then \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" But if points from dropped clusters are reassigned, they would have new exit_lambdas? The input gives point_exit_lambdas per cluster, which are the lambdas at which each point exits that cluster (i.e., leaves the cluster). For a child cluster, its points will exit at some lambda. When the child is dropped, those points are considered part of the parent, and their exit lambda from the parent is actually the child's birth lambda? Or they are considered to have exited the parent at the same lambda? Need to interpret.\n\nThe typical HDBSCAN stability selection: After building the condensed tree, clusters with size < min_cluster_size are \"condensed\" into their parent. The points from the condensed cluster are assigned to the parent, and the parent's lambda values for those points become the child's birth lambda (since they join the parent at that lambda) and the child's exit lambdas are ignored? Actually, the standard algorithm for computing stability in HDBSCAN (as per the paper) when condensing: for each point, we track its birth lambda (when it first became part of a cluster that survives condensing) and its death lambda (when it leaves the cluster or when the cluster splits). For a point that is in a child cluster that is below min_cluster_size, that child cluster is considered \"noise\" and the point is assigned to the parent with birth lambda equal to the child's birth lambda (i.e., when it entered the child) and death lambda equal to the point's exit lambda from the child? Or does the point die at the child's birth lambda? Actually, in HDBSCAN, clusters below min_cluster_size are considered as \"noise\" and their points are considered to have died at the lambda where they would have left the parent? Let's recall: The condensed tree is built by taking the hierarchy and removing all clusters with a smaller number of points than min_cluster_size. Points in those removed clusters are then considered to have \"fallen out\" of the hierarchy at that lambda. The stability of a cluster is the sum over all points in the cluster (after condensation) of (death_lambda - birth_lambda). For a point that remains in a cluster that never splits further, death_lambda is the lambda at which the cluster itself ends (its parent's birth lambda or infinity). For a point that is in a cluster that splits, death_lambda is the lambda of the split (i.e., the birth lambda of the child cluster). But here the input provides per-cluster point_exit_lambdas, which likely represent the lambdas at which each point exits that cluster (to go to a child or to noise). And birth_lambda is the lambda at which the cluster appears (i.e., its points converge). The parent mapping tells the hierarchical structure.\n\nGiven that, the condensation process: For each cluster with size < min_cluster_size, it and all its points are not considered as separate clusters. Those points are instead attributed to the parent cluster. However, the points' exit lambdas from the parent are not directly given; we must compute them from the hierarchy. In standard HDBSCAN, points move up to the parent, and their death lambda becomes the birth lambda of the child they were originally in (since they leave the parent cluster at that lambda). Actually, let's think: In the hierarchy, each point starts at leaf level (highest lambda). As lambda decreases, points merge. Each cluster has a birth lambda (lambda at which it forms). When a parent splits into children, the children have birth lambdas higher than the parent's birth. The points in a child have an exit lambda from the parent equal to the child's birth lambda (the point leaves the parent to join the child). So if a child is condensed (removed), the point does not actually leave the parent; it stays in the parent until it either leaves to another child or the parent ends. But the point may have its own exit lambda from the child (given as point_exit_lambdas) which indicates when it leaves the child. If the child is removed, that exit is irrelevant; the point remains in the parent until it would have left the parent (maybe at a later lambda). However, the simplified approach common in HDBSCAN library (like hdbscan) when computing stability after condensation: For each point, we track its birth lambda as the lambda of the highest ancestor cluster that survived condensing, and its death lambda as the lambda at which it would have left that ancestor (i.e., the birth lambda of the first child that was condensed or the point's exit from the child if the child survived? Actually, the algorithm is more complex.\n\nGiven the problem statement is a bounded objective, we need to infer the intended behavior from the provided inputs. There are three identical fixtures with same data but different nonces. The input clusters: c0 size 7, birth_lambda 0.2, point_exit_lambdas [0.4, 0.6, 0.9] (three points? Wait size 7 but only 3 exit lambdas? That seems inconsistent. Possibly each point_exit_lambda corresponds to a point in that cluster? But size 7 would imply 7 points. The given list has 3 values. Perhaps the point_exit_lambdas are only for the points that actually exit via that cluster? In HDBSCAN, each cluster has a list of points that exit that specific cluster (to go to a child or to noise). The cluster size includes all points that belong to that cluster (including those that will later exit to children). The cluster's own points (the ones that stay until the cluster ends) are not listed? Alternatively, the input may be a simplified representation. Let's examine the hierarchy: parent mapping: c0 has no parent (null), c1 child of c0, c2 child of c0. birth_lambda: c0=0.2, c1=0.5, c2=0.8. So c0 births at 0.2, then at lambda 0.5, c1 splits off, so some points leave c0 to form c1. At lambda 0.8, c2 splits off. c1 has point_exit_lambdas [0.7,1.0,1.2] – these could be when points leave c1 (maybe to further children or to noise). Similarly c2 has [1.1]. c0 also has point_exit_lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9] – these could be points that leave c0 directly (not to children). But since children exist, the points that leave at 0.5 and 0.8 (births of children) are not in c0's point_exit_lambdas? Possibly those points are accounted in the child clusters. Typically in condensed tree, each point has a series of birth and death events. The input format might be: each cluster contains the birth_lambda and a list of exit lambdas for points that are in that cluster and exit it (i.e., the cluster's own \"points\" that are not assigned to any child). The size is total points assigned to that cluster including those that will go to children? But c0 size 7 but sum of point_exit_lambdas lengths: c0 has 3, c1 has 3, c2 has 1 = 7. So total points accounted. So each cluster's size equals the number of points that have that cluster as their \"last\" cluster before they exit? Or the number of points that have their ultimate death in that cluster? Actually, in the condensed tree each point belongs to exactly one leaf cluster (the most specific) and then goes up the hierarchy. The point_exit_lambdas for a cluster represent the lambdas at which points that are in that cluster exit it to go to parent? But the point_exit_lambdas for child clusters would be the lambdas at which points leave the child back to parent (i.e., the point's death lambda relative to the child). The point's birth lambda in that child is the child's birth lambda. So stability for a cluster = sum over its points of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda). For c1: birth=0.5, exits: 0.7,1.0,1.2 -> stabilities: 0.2,0.5,0.7 -> sum=1.4. For c2: birth=0.8, exit 1.1 -> stability=0.3. For c0: birth=0.2, exits:0.4,0.6,0.9 -> stabilities:0.2,0.4,0.7 -> sum=1.3. But c0 also inherits points from children if children are condensed? Possibly.\n\nNow condensation: min_cluster_size=3. c0 size 7 >=3, retained. c1 size 4 >=3, retained? Wait 4>=3, so retained. c2 size 2 <3, dropped. So we drop c2. What happens to c2's points? They must be reassigned to c0 (its parent). But c0's point_exit_lambdas do not include those points. The points that were in c2 have birth at 0.8 (c2's birth) and exit at 1.1 (given). If we drop c2, those points should be considered as part of c0. Their stability contribution to c0: they enter c0 at lambda 0.2? Actually, they were in c0 before c2 formed (at lambda 0.2). At lambda 0.8, they left c0 to form c2. But if c2 is dropped, that split is ignored; they never left c0. So they remain in c0 until they eventually exit c0. But what is their exit lambda from c0? It would be the lambda at which they would have left c2 (1.1) but since c2 is merged back, they exit c0 directly at 1.1? Or they exit c0 at the same lambda as c2's birth (0.8)? The standard HDBSCAN: when condensing, points from condensed clusters are considered to have died at the cluster's birth lambda (i.e., they are added to the parent cluster but with death lambda equal to the child's birth lambda). But that would mean the point exits the parent at the child's birth lambda, which seems to reduce stability. However, the alternative where they are considered to have died at the point's original death lambda might be more intuitive. Let's look at the common Python library hdbscan: The condensed tree is built by removing clusters below min_cluster_size. Each point's birth and death lambda are recorded. For points in a removed cluster, their death lambda is set to the birth lambda of the parent (i.e., the lambda at which they would have been in the parent). The point's birth lambda is the lambda of the highest surviving ancestor. So stability of a surviving cluster is sum over points of (death_birth_diff). That is how it's done.\n\nGiven that, let's compute for this example with min_cluster_size=3. Retain c0 and c1. Drop c2. c2's points: there is one point (since c2 has 1 exit lambda). Its birth in c0: 0.2 (since it was in c0 from start). It went to c2 at 0.8 (c2 birth). If c2 is dropped, that point is considered to have died at 0.8 (the child's birth lambda). So its death lambda = 0.8. Its stability contribution to c0 is (0.8 - 0.2)=0.6. So c0's total stability: original sum from its own points (1.3) plus contribution from dropped child = 0.6 -> 1.9. But we also need to consider that c0's own points might also be affected? The original c0's point_exit_lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9] are still valid because those points exit directly from c0 (not via children). However, the point that exited at 0.9 might be one of the points that later? Actually, the points count: c0 has 3 points that exit directly, c1 has 3 points, c2 has 1 point. Total 7. c1 is retained, so its points stay in c1. But c1 is a child of c0. In the condensed tree, c1 remains as a separate cluster because its size >=3. So points in c1 have birth at 0.5 (c1 birth) and exit at given lambdas. They are not part of c0's stability. So c0's stability only includes points that are in c0 after condensing out c2? Actually, points in c1 are separate; they are in c1. Their stability contributes to c1. c0's stability includes only the points that stay in c0 (i.e., the ones that never go to a retained child) plus points from condensed children. So points that go to c1 are not in c0's stability. So we